  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer today and Monday, with disturbances \narriving that will trigger showers and a few thunderstorms.\n\nSurface observations indicate that high pressure that has been \nsettled over the Mid-Atlantic over the past few days continues \nto gradually weaken. Meanwhile, a large dome of high pressure \ncentered near the Four Corners region of the southwestern United\nStates continues to build, allowing the overall windflow across\nthe lower Mid-Atlantic to shift from the northwest. This will \nallow for increased thunderstorm chances during the early half \nof the week as disturbances approach our area from the Great \nLakes region.\n\nConfidence is increasing that one such disturbance will arrive \nduring early this evening, triggering thunderstorms that will \ndevelop along the Blue Ridge north of Roanoke, and pass as a\nbroken line southeast across the Piedmont through midnight. \nWith stronger heating today as highs range from the upper 70s to\nthe mid 80s, believe there will be sufficient energy in the \nform of CAPE ranging from 1,000 to 1,200 J/kg that storms may \noccasionally intensify to severe levels, and become capable of \nproducing localized damaging wind gusts. Outside of that, other\nweak disturbances today through Monday will trigger additional \nisolated to widely scattered showers and a few storms, which are\nforecasted to remain weak and disorganized. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Confidence increasing of organized strong to \nsevere thunderstorms arriving late Monday night, which will \nredevelop across the area on Tuesday.\n\nThe potential for severe thunderstorms will increase late \nMonday night as a strong upper level disturbance passing near \nthe Great Lakes triggers a line of intense thunderstorm \nactivity. Confidence is increasing that this line will push \nsouthward across West Virginia late Monday night, reaching the \nInterstate 64 corridor (west of Lexington) during the 1 AM to 5 \nAM timeframe early Tuesday. It's uncertain how intense the line \nof storms will be as a whole given arrival in the middle of the \nnight, but have high confidence that a few storms within the \nline will still be capable of damaging wind gusts. Believe as \nthe storms cross the higher terrain of southeast West Virginia, \nthey will then weaken in downslope windflow. Latest data \nsuggest that the line will continue to advance south across our\narea through the remainder of the night, and will then have to \nwatch for the potential of flash flooding should the line stall,\nallowing storm cells to pass repeatedly across the same \nlocations.\n\nAnother strong disturbance will arrive on Tuesday, and will \nsupport the redevelopment of scattered thunderstorms which, \nagain, will have the potential to produce damaging winds. Much \nabout how Tuesday afternoon's storms evolve may depend on where \noutflow boundaries and residual cloud cover are located leftover\nfrom the early morning activity. Regardless, the strength of \nthe upper level disturbance crossing the central Appalachians \nappears sufficient that individual cells forming during early\nafternoon may solidify into a broken line of strong to severe \nstorms somewhere across our area by late afternoon into the \nevening.\n\nThe severe thunderstorm threat will end Tuesday evening as the\nstorms exit south and east of our area, with a trailing cold\nfront then making its way southward across the Mid-Atlantic on\nTuesday night.",
